: KEY ( --- c )
\ Receive one character from the terminal.
\
\ One byte, unbuffered, straight off descriptor 0 - which is what
\ gforth, SPF and lbForth all do, and what dash does for its `read`
\ builtin. SPF's is character for character this:
\
\     : KEY-TERMIOS   0 SP@ 1 H-STDIN READ-FILE DROP DROP ;
\
\ No buffer, and no buffer is possible: descriptor 0 is INHERITED,
\ shared with every child, and a byte read ahead is a byte taken from
\ whatever runs next. The engine used to hold 4096 bytes of it, and
\ `read x; cat` swallowed the whole pipe - see tests/shell/run-read.
\
\ Reading into the cell the 0 just pushed, via SP@, for the same
\ reason EMIT does: the character is already in memory, so no
\ variable is needed to hold it.
\ KNOWN SHORTCUT, see GOALS.md: this tests the COUNT and drops the
\ ior, so a non-blocking descriptor with no data yet - which also
\ returns a count of 0 - makes KEY exit. Not reachable until something
\ sets O_NONBLOCK on descriptor 0, which is what implementing KEY?
\ will do. Fixing it properly needs an fcntl primitive.
\
\ EOF exits, which is not decoration: the old primitive did it in C,
\ and dropping it here reintroduced GOALS.md's Bug 3 within minutes -
\ a shell reading a script off stdin spun on read(0,"",1)=0 forever,
\ 2.7 million times before the test timed out.
   0 SP@ 1 0 READ-FILE DROP
   0= IF BYE THEN ;

: ACCEPT ( c-addr n1 --- n2 )
\ Read a line from the terminal into a buffer at c-addr of length n1;
\ n2 is the number of characters read. Backspace and DEL erase one
\ character if there is one, CR and LF end the line and are not
\ stored, and a character that would overflow the buffer is dropped.
\
\ ANS requires this to WAIT for a line terminator, which is why it
\ cannot be the non-blocking read; that is READ-STDIN, and the standard's
\ own answer for "read whatever is ready" is KEY? - which this system
\ does not have yet.
  >R 0
  BEGIN
   KEY DUP 8 = OVER 127 = OR
   IF
     DROP DUP IF 1- THEN
   ELSE
     DUP 10 = OVER 13 = OR
     IF
      DROP SWAP DROP R> DROP EXIT
     ELSE
      OVER R@ - IF
       >R OVER OVER + R> SWAP C! 1+
      ELSE
       DROP
      THEN
     THEN
   THEN
  0 UNTIL
;

: QUERY ( --- )
\ Read a line from the terminal into the terminal input buffer.
  TIB 80 ACCEPT #TIB ! ;

: SOURCE ( --- addr len)
\ Return the address and length of the current input source.
   SRC @ #SRC @ ;

: SOURCE-ID ( --- sid)
\ Return the i.d. of the current source i.d., 0 for terminal, -1
\ for EVALUATE and positive number for INCLUDE file.
   SID @ ;

: REFILL ( --- f)
\ Refill the current input source when it is exhausted. f is
\ true if it was successfully refilled.
  SOURCE-ID -1 = IF
   0 \ Not refillable for EVALUATE
  ELSE
   SOURCE-ID IF
    SRC @ 256 SOURCE-ID READ-LINE ABORT" Error reading file"
    SWAP #SRC ! 0 >IN !
    #SRC @ IF SOURCE OVER + SWAP 
    BEGIN 2DUP - WHILE DUP C@ 9 = IF BL OVER C! THEN 1+ REPEAT 2DROP THEN
    \ Change tabs to space.
    \ flag from READ-LINE is returned (no success at EOF)
   ELSE
    QUERY #TIB @ #SRC ! 0 >IN ! -1 \ Always successful from terminal.
   THEN
  THEN
;

: SCAN ( c-addr1 u1 c --- c-addr2 u2 )
\ Find the first occurrence of character c in the string c-addr1 u1
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remaining part of the string starting with that char.
\ It is a zero-length string if c was not found.
  BEGIN
   OVER
  WHILE
   ROT DUP C@ >R OVER R> =
   IF
    ROT ROT DROP EXIT
   THEN
   1+ ROT ROT SWAP 1- SWAP
  REPEAT DROP
;

: SKIP ( c-addr1 u1 c --- c-addr2 u2 )
\ Find the first character not equal to c in the string c-addr1 u1
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remaining part of the string starting with the
\ nonmatching char. It is a zero-length string if no other chars found.
  BEGIN
   OVER
  WHILE
   ROT DUP C@ >R OVER R> -
   IF
    ROT ROT DROP EXIT
   THEN
   1+ ROT ROT SWAP 1- SWAP
  REPEAT DROP
;

: PARSE ( c --- addr len )
\ Find a character sequence in the current source that is delimited by
\ character c. Adjust >IN to 1 past the end delimiter character.
  >R SOURCE >IN @ - SWAP >IN @ + R> OVER >R >R SWAP
  R@ SKIP OVER R> SWAP >R SCAN IF 1 >IN +! THEN
  DUP R@ - R> SWAP
  ROT R> - >IN +! ;

: PLACE ( addr len c-addr --- )
\ Place the string starting at addr with length len at c-addr as
\ a counted string.
  2DUP C! CHAR+ SWAP CMOVE ;

: WORD ( c --- addr )
\ Parse a character sequence delimited by character c and return the
\ address of a counted string that is a copy of it. The counted
\ string is actually placed at HERE. The character after the counted
\ string is set to a space.
  PARSE HERE PLACE HERE BL HERE COUNT + C! ;

\ PART 8: INTERPRETER HELPER WORDS

\ First we need FIND and related words.

\ Each word list consists of one linked list of definitions. No hashing
\ is used to speed up dictionary search. All names in the dictionary
\ are at aligned addresses and FIND is optimized to compare one 4-byte
\ cell at a time.

\ Dictionary definitions are built as follows:
\
\ LINK field: 1 cell, aligned, contains name field of previous word in list.
\ NAME field: counted string of at most 31 characters.
\             bits 5-7 of length byte have special meaning.
\                   7 is always set to mark start of name ( for >NAME)
\                   6 is set if the word is immediate.
\                   5 is set if the word is a macro.
\ CODE field: first aligned address after name, is execution token for word.
\             here the executable code for the word starts. (is 1 cell for
\             variables etc.)
\ PARAMETER field: (body) Contains the data of constants and variables etc.

VARIABLE NAMEBUF ( --- a-addr)
\ An aligned buffer that holds a copy of the name that is searched.
28 ALLOT-T

VARIABLE WORDLISTS ( --- a-addr)
\ Head of the chain of wordlists OTHER than FORTH-WORDLIST: the
\ START-relative offset of the most recently created one, or 0.
\
\ Each such wordlist carries the offset of the next in a link cell
\ immediately BEFORE its count cell, so the chain costs one cell per
\ wordlist and needs no table and no limit. Offsets, not addresses, so
\ the chain itself never needs relocating - only the thread heads
\ inside each wordlist do.
\
\ COLD relocates every wordlist on this chain and SAVE-SYSTEM
\ unrelocates the same walk. Before it existed both knew about exactly
\ one wordlist, which was complete for as long as there WAS one: the
\ moment extend.4 was loaded into a saved image, ROOT-WORDLIST and
\ every VOCABULARY kept the builder's absolute addresses, and two
\ identical builds differed in eight cells by exactly the ASLR delta.

VARIABLE FORTH-WORDLIST ( --- addr)
\ A hashed word list, laid out as SOD32's is: cell 0 holds the number
\ of threads and cells 1..n the head of each thread.
\
\ RelF carried this structure over from SOD32 as a SINGLE cell - "a
\ pointer to the last definition" - which made every lookup a linear
\ scan of the whole dictionary. Measured on bench/parse.fth that cost
\ 8.05 million dictionary entries visited against SOD32's 397 thousand,
\ and about 81% of every operation the system executed. See
\ FINDINGS-OUTER-INTERPRETER.md.
32 CELLS-T ALLOT-T

VARIABLE LAST ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds a pointer to the last definition created.

VARIABLE CONTEXT 28 ALLOT-T ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable holds the addresses of up to 8 word lists that are
\ in the search order.

VARIABLE #ORDER ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the number of word list that are in the search order.

VARIABLE CURRENT ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the address of the word list to which new definitions
\ are added.

: HASH ( c-addr u #threads --- n )
\ SOD32's hash, unchanged: the length and the first two characters.
\ It must agree EXACTLY with cross.4's THASH, which threads the
\ dictionary of the image being built, or a word would be filed under
\ one thread and looked for under another.
  >R OVER C@ 1 LSHIFT OVER 1 > IF ROT 1+ C@ 2 LSHIFT XOR ELSE ROT DROP
   THEN XOR
  R> 1- AND ;

: NAME>BUF ( c-addr u ---)
\ Move the name c-addr u to the aligned buffer NAMEBUF.
  NAMEBUF 32 0 FILL 32 MIN NAMEBUF PLACE ;

: SEARCH-WORDLIST ( c-addr u wid --- 0 | xt 1 xt -1)
\ Search the wordlist with address wid for the name c-addr u.
\ Return 0 if not found, the execution token xt and -1 for non-immediate
\ words and xt and 1 for immediate words.
  ROT ROT
  NAME>BUF
  \ Pick the thread this name would have been filed under. Everything
  \ below is unchanged: the chain walk, the cell-at-a-time compare and
  \ the relative link field are all as they were.
  DUP >R NAMEBUF COUNT R@ @ HASH 1+ CELLS + @ R> DROP
  DUP IF
  BEGIN
   \ Mask clears the flag bits within the count byte - which, since cells
   \ are read native-endian (little-endian on every host targeted so
   \ far - see GOALS.md phase 5), is the LOW byte of the cell, not the
   \ high byte the old big-endian-packed mask (1 61 LSHIFT 1 -) assumed.
   NAMEBUF @ OVER @ -1 224 XOR AND = \ Compare first cells of names.
           \ Mask off bits 5-7 of name length, these have special purposes.
   IF
    NAMEBUF CELL+
    OVER CELL+
    CELLBYTES-TOK >R
    BEGIN
     OVER @ OVER @ - >R
     CELL+ SWAP CELL+ SWAP R> R> CELL+ >R \ Compare all of name until different
    UNTIL
    R> NAMEBUF C@ 1 + ALIGNED CELL+ = IF \ match to the right length then found
     SWAP DROP SWAP C@
     64 AND IF \ determine immediateness of a word from bit 6 of name length.
      1
     ELSE
      -1
     THEN
     SWAP CELLBYTES-TOK - SWAP EXIT \ Exit with execution token and flag.
    THEN
    2DROP
   THEN
   CELLBYTES-TOK - DUP @ DUP ROT ROT + SWAP 0 =  \ Take address of next word from link field until 0
  UNTIL
  THEN
  DROP 0 \ Not found.
;

: FIND ( c-addr --- c-addr 0| xt 1|xt -1 )
\ Search all word lists in the search order for the name in the
\ counted string at c-addr. If not found return the name address and 0.
\ If found return the execution token xt and -1 if the word is non-immediate
\ and 1 if the word is immediate.
  #ORDER @ DUP 1 > IF
   CONTEXT #ORDER @ 1- CELLS + DUP @ SWAP CELLBYTES-TOK - @ =
  ELSE 0 THEN
  IF 1- THEN \ If last wordlist is double, don't search it twice.
  BEGIN
   DUP
  WHILE
   1- >R
   DUP COUNT
   R@ CELLS CONTEXT + @ SEARCH-WORDLIST
   DUP
   IF
    R> DROP ROT DROP EXIT \ Exit if found.
   THEN
   DROP R>
  REPEAT
;

\ The following words are related to numeric input.

: DIGIT? ( c -- 0| c--- n -1)
\ Convert character c to its digit value n and return true if c is a
\ digit in the current base. Otherwise return false.
  48 - DUP 0< IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 9 > OVER 17 < AND IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 9 > IF 7 - THEN
  DUP BASE @ < 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  -1
;

: >NUMBER ( ud1 c-addr1 u1 --- ud2 c-addr2 u2 )
\ Convert the string at c-addr with length u1 to binary, multiplying ud1
\ by the number in BASE and adding the digit value to it for each digit.
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remainder of the string starting at the first character
\ that is no digit.
  BEGIN
   DUP
  WHILE
   -1 + >R
   COUNT DIGIT? 0=
   IF
    R> 1+ SWAP -1 + SWAP  EXIT
   THEN
   SWAP >R
   >R
   SWAP BASE @ UM* ROT BASE @ * 0 SWAP D+ \ Multiply ud by base.
   R> 0 D+                                \ Add new digit.
   R> R>
  REPEAT
;

: CONVERT ( ud1 c-addr1 --- ud2 c-addr2)
\ Convert the string starting at c-addr1 + 1 to binary. c-addr2 is the
\ address of the first non-digit. Digits are added into ud1 as in >NUMBER
  -1 + -1 >NUMBER DROP ;

: NUMBER? ( c-addr ---- d f)
\ Convert the counted string at c-addr to a double binary number.
\ f is true if and only if the conversion was successful. DPL contains
\ -1 if there was no point in the number, else the position of the point
\ from the right. Special prefixes: # means decimal, $ means hex.
  -1 DPL !
  BASE @ >R
  COUNT
  OVER C@ 45 = DUP >R IF    -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN \ Get any - sign
  OVER C@ 36 = IF 16 BASE ! -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ $ sign for hex.
  OVER C@ 35 = IF 10 BASE ! -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ # sign for decimal
  DUP  0 > 0= IF  R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Length 0 or less?
  >R >R 0 0 R> R>
  BEGIN
   >NUMBER
   DUP IF OVER C@ 46 = IF -1 + DUP DPL ! SWAP 1 + SWAP ELSE \ handle point.
         R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Error if anything but point
       THEN
  DUP 0= UNTIL 2DROP R> IF DNEGATE THEN
  R> BASE ! -1
;

\ PART 9: THE COMPILER

: (ABORT") ( f --- )
\ Runtime part of ABORT"
           IF R> COUNT TYPE SPACE HERE COUNT TYPE CR WARM
           ELSE R> COUNT + ALIGNED >R THEN ;


: ALLOT ( n --- )
\ Allot n extra bytes of memory, starting at HERE to the dictionary.
  DP +! ;

: , ( x --- )
\ Append cell x to the dictionary at HERE.
  HERE ! 1 CELLS ALLOT ;

: C, ( n --- )
\ Append character c to the dictionary at HERE.
  HERE C! 1 ALLOT ;

: ALIGN ( --- )
\ Add as many bytes to the dictionary as needed to align dictionary pointer.
  BEGIN HERE CELLBYTES-TOK 1- AND WHILE 0 C, REPEAT ;

: >NAME ( addr1 --- addr2 )
\ Convert execution token addr1 (address of code) to address of name.
  BEGIN 1- DUP C@ 128 AND UNTIL ;

: NAME> ( addr1 --- addr2 )
\ Convert address of name to address of code.
  COUNT 31 AND + ALIGNED ;

: HEADER ( --- )
\ Create a header for a new definition without a code field.
  ALIGN
  \ The link field cannot be filled in yet: which thread this word
  \ belongs to depends on its name, and the name has not been parsed.
  \ So reserve the cell, remember where it is, and patch it below -
  \ which is what SOD32 does.
  HERE >R 0 ,
  HERE LAST !         \ Set LAST so definition can be linked by REVEAL
  32 WORD DUP FIND IF ." Redefining: " HERE COUNT TYPE CR THEN DROP
                       \ Give warning if existing word redefined.
  DUP COUNT CURRENT @ @ HASH 1+ CELLS CURRENT @ + @
  ?DUP IF R@ - R@ ! THEN    \ link, relative to the link cell itself
  R> DROP
  C@ 1+ HERE C@ 128 + HERE C! ALLOT ALIGN
                       \ Allot the name and set bit 7 in length byte.
;

: REVEAL ( --- )
\ Add the last created definition to the CURRENT wordlist, at the head
\ of the thread its name hashes to.
  LAST @ DUP COUNT 31 AND CURRENT @ @ HASH
  1+ CELLS CURRENT @ + ! ;



: OP, ( n --- )
\ Append one opcode byte.
  C, ;

VARIABLE LAST-OP ( --- a-addr )
\ Where the last foldable operation starts, or 0.
VARIABLE LAST-LIT ( --- a-addr )
\ Where the last literal starts, or 0...
VARIABLE LAST-LITV ( --- a-addr )
\ ...and its value.
VARIABLE LAST-CALL ( --- a-addr )
\ Where the last call starts, or 0...
VARIABLE LAST-CALLXT ( --- a-addr )
\ ...and its target.

\ The compiler rewrites four two-operation patterns into one opcode
\ (CV8-REFERENCE.md 7): `LIT n +` and `LIT n =` into ADDI/EQI, and
\ `var @` and `var !` into VAR@/VAR!. It remembers where the last
\ literal and the last call started, and the second half of a pattern
\ rewinds HERE over the first. NOTHING MAY BRANCH BETWEEN THE TWO, so
\ everything that makes HERE a branch target, or emits anything that is
\ not one of the halves, calls NO-PEEP. `;` folds a final operation and
\ its EXIT into one opcode under the same rule.
: NO-PEEP ( --- )
  0 LAST-OP ! 0 LAST-LIT ! 0 LAST-CALL ! ;

: REWIND ( a-addr --- )
\ Discard everything compiled from a-addr to HERE.
  HERE - ALLOT ;

: W, ( n --- )
\ Append a 16-bit little-endian operand.
  DUP 255 AND C, 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, ;

: SLOT, ( a-addr --- )
\ A slot operand naming a-addr: a scaled offset like a call, two bytes
\ big-endian if it fits 15 bits, else three with the top bit set.
  START @ - CELLSHIFT-TOK RSHIFT
  DUP 32768 < IF DUP 8 RSHIFT C, 255 AND C,
  ELSE DUP 16 RSHIFT 128 OR C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 255 AND C, THEN ;

VARIABLE STATE ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that holds the compiler state, 0 is interpreting 1 is compiling.

: ]  ( --- )
\ Start compilation mode.
  1 STATE ! ;

: [  ( --- )
\ Leave compilation mode.
  0 STATE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: LIT, ( n --- )
\ Append a literal in the narrowest form that holds it.
  NO-PEEP HERE LAST-LIT ! DUP LAST-LITV !
  DUP 0 = IF DROP 97 OP, EXIT THEN              \ push 0
  DUP 1 = IF DROP 98 OP, EXIT THEN              \ push 1
  DUP -1 = IF DROP 99 OP, EXIT THEN             \ push -1
  DUP 256 U< IF HERE LAST-OP ! LIT8-TOK OP, C, EXIT THEN
  DUP 65536 U< IF HERE LAST-OP ! [OP] LIT OP, W, EXIT THEN
  \ A 64-bit cell holds values a signed 32-bit field cannot.
  DUP 2147483648 + 4294967296 U< CELLBYTES-TOK 4 = OR IF
    LIT32-TOK OP, DUP W, 16 RSHIFT W, EXIT
  THEN
  125 OP, DUP DUP W, 16 RSHIFT W, 32 RSHIFT DUP W, 16 RSHIFT W, ;

: LITERAL ( n --- )
\ Add a literal to the current definition.
  LIT, ; IMMEDIATE

VARIABLE FOLD-OPS ( --- a-addr )
\ The primitives with a folded "then EXIT" opcode, as opcode bytes, in
\ the order of the engine's table.
FOLD-TABLE

: FOLD-OP ( op --- op2 -1 | op 0 )
\ The folded opcode for op, if it has one.
  DUP LIT8-TOK = IF DROP LIT8X-TOK -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 121 = OVER 123 = OR IF 1+ -1 EXIT THEN   \ ADDI, EQI
  #FOLD-TOK 0 DO
    DUP FOLD-OPS I + C@ = IF DROP I FOLD-BASE-TOK + -1 UNLOOP EXIT THEN
  LOOP 0 ;

: EXIT, ( --- )
\ Append a return, folded into the last operation where that has a
\ folded form. Only ever at the END of a body, so nothing can branch to
\ the EXIT that folding removes. A final 0 or 1 becomes LIT8 so that it
\ folds too.
  LAST-LIT @ IF LAST-LITV @ 2 U< IF
    LAST-LIT @ REWIND HERE LAST-OP ! LIT8-TOK OP, LAST-LITV @ C,
  THEN THEN
  LAST-OP @ ?DUP IF
    DUP C@ FOLD-OP IF SWAP C! ELSE 2DROP [OP] EXIT OP, THEN
  ELSE [OP] EXIT OP, THEN
  NO-PEEP ;

: CALL, ( a-addr --- )
\ A call to the body at a-addr: a scaled offset from the image base,
\ two bytes if it fits 14 bits, else three.
  NO-PEEP HERE LAST-CALL ! DUP LAST-CALLXT !
  START @ - CELLSHIFT-TOK RSHIFT
  DUP 16384 < IF DUP 8 RSHIFT 128 OR C, 255 AND C,
  ELSE DUP 16 RSHIFT 192 OR C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 255 AND C, THEN ;

: PEEP-IMM ( op --- f )
\ Rewrite `LIT n +` or `LIT n =` just compiled, if op completes it.
  LAST-LIT @ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LAST-LITV @ 128 + 256 U< 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP [OP] + = IF DROP 121 ELSE
  [OP] = = IF 123 ELSE 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-LIT @ REWIND LAST-LITV @ >R NO-PEEP
  HERE LAST-OP ! OP, R> 255 AND C, -1 ;

: PEEP-VAR ( op --- f )
\ Rewrite `var @` or `var !` just compiled, where var is a plain data
\ word: its body starts with DOVAR, so its parameter field is fixed.
  LAST-CALL @ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LAST-CALLXT @ C@ DOVAR-TOK = 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP [OP] @ = IF DROP 100 ELSE
  [OP] ! = IF 101 ELSE 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-CALL @ REWIND LAST-CALLXT @ >R NO-PEEP
  OP, R> CELL+ SLOT, -1 ;   \ >BODY, which is defined later

: COMPILE, ( xt --- )
\ Add the execution semantics of xt to the current definition. A word
\ with the MACRO bit is a primitive or an opcode word: its body starts
\ with its opcode, or with ESC and a selector, and that is inlined.
\ Anything else is a call.
  DUP >NAME C@ 32 AND IF
    DUP C@ 126 = IF
      NO-PEEP DUP C@ OP, 1+ C@ OP,
    ELSE
      C@ DUP PEEP-IMM IF DROP EXIT THEN
      DUP PEEP-VAR IF DROP EXIT THEN
      NO-PEEP HERE LAST-OP ! OP,
    THEN
  ELSE
    CALL,
  THEN ;

VARIABLE CSP ( --- a-addr )
\ This variable is used for stack checking between : and ;

VARIABLE 'LEAVE ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable is used for LEAVE address resolution.

: !CSP ( --- )
\ Store current stack pointer in CSP.
   SP@ CSP ! ;

: ?CSP ( --- )
\ Check that stack pointer is equal to value contained in CSP.
   SP@ CSP @ - ABORT" Incomplete control structure" ;

: ; ( --- )
\ Finish the current definition by adding a return to it, make it
\ visible and leave compilation mode.
  EXIT, [ ?CSP REVEAL ; IMMEDIATE

: CREATE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Create a definition that returns its parameter field address when
\ executed. Storage can be added to it with ALLOT. The body is
\ [DOVAR][pad to a cell]: the pad has room for DOES> to overwrite the
\ front with [DODOES][call], and the parameter field does not move.
  HEADER REVEAL NO-PEEP DOVAR-TOK OP, ALIGN ;

: VARIABLE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Create a variable where 1 cell can be stored. When executed it
\ returns the address.
  CREATE 0 , ;

: CONSTANT ( x "ccc" ---)
\ Create a definition that returns x when executed.
  HEADER REVEAL LIT, EXIT, ;

: (POSTPONE) ( --- )
\ Runtime for POSTPONE.
\ has inline argument.
  R> DUP DUP @ + SWAP CELL+ >R
  DUP >NAME C@ 64 AND IF EXECUTE ELSE COMPILE, THEN
;

: : ( "ccc" --- )
\ Start a new definition, enter compilation mode.
  !CSP HEADER ] ;

: >MARK ( --- a-addr )
\ Emit a placeholder branch operand and leave its address. A branch
\ operand is a signed 16-bit offset from the operand itself.
  NO-PEEP HERE 0 W, ;

: >RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
\ Patch the operand at a-addr so its branch lands on HERE.
  NO-PEEP HERE OVER - SWAP 2DUP C! 1+ SWAP 8 RSHIFT SWAP C! ;

: <RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
\ Emit a branch operand jumping back to a-addr.
  NO-PEEP HERE - W, ;

: BEGIN ( --- x )
\ Start a BEGIN UNTIL or BEGIN WHILE REPEAT loop.
  NO-PEEP HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: UNTIL ( x --- )
\ Form a loop with matching BEGIN.
\ Runtime: A flag is take from the stack
\ each time UNTIL is encountered and the loop iterates until it is nonzero.
  NO-PEEP [OP] ?BRANCH OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: AGAIN ( x --- )
\ End a BEGIN AGAIN loop: branch back unconditionally.
  NO-PEEP [OP] BRANCH OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: IF    ( --- x)
\ Start an IF THEN or IF ELSE THEN construction.
\ Runtime: At IF a flag is taken from
\ the stack and if it is true the part between IF and ELSE is executed,
\ otherwise the part between ELSE and THEN. If there is no ELSE, the part
\ between IF and THEN is executed only if flag is true.
  NO-PEEP [OP] ?BRANCH OP, >MARK ; IMMEDIATE

: THEN ( x ---)
\ End an IF THEN or IF ELSE THEN construction.
  >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: ELSE ( x1 --- x2)
\ part of IF ELSE THEN construction.
  NO-PEEP [OP] BRANCH OP, >MARK SWAP >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: WHILE  ( x1 --- x2 x1 )
\ part of BEGIN WHILE REPEAT construction.
\ Runtime: At WHILE a flag is taken from the stack. If it is false,
\  the program jumps out of the loop, otherwise the part between WHILE
\  and REPEAT is executed and the loop iterates to BEGIN.
  POSTPONE IF SWAP ; IMMEDIATE

: REPEAT ( x1 x2 --- )
\ part of BEGIN WHILE REPEAT construction.
  NO-PEEP [OP] BRANCH OP, <RESOLVE >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

VARIABLE POCKET ( --- a-addr )
\ Buffer for S" strings that are interpreted.
  252 ALLOT-T

: '  ( "ccc" --- xt)
\ Find the word with name ccc and return its execution token.
  32 WORD FIND 0= ABORT" Not found" ;

: ['] ( "ccc" ---)
\ Compile the execution token of the word with name ccc as a literal.
  ' LITERAL ; IMMEDIATE

: CHAR ( "ccc" --- c)
\ Return the first character of "ccc".
  BL WORD 1 + C@ ;

: [CHAR] ( "ccc" --- )
\ Compile the first character of "ccc" as a literal.
  CHAR LITERAL ; IMMEDIATE

: OPERAND-ALIGN ( --- )
\ (LOOP), (+LOOP), (?DO), (LEAVE) and (POSTPONE) are followed by an
\ inline CELL, read with @. Pad with NOOPs so that it is aligned: they
\ execute, once, before the call. Calls to kernel words are two bytes.
  BEGIN HERE 2 + CELLBYTES-TOK 1- AND WHILE 0 C, REPEAT ;

: DO ( --- x)
\ Start a DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: ( n1 n2 --- ) start a loop with initial count n2 and
\ limit n1.
  POSTPONE (DO) NO-PEEP 'LEAVE @ HERE 0 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: ?DO ( --- x )
\ Start a ?DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: ( n1 n2 --- ) start a loop with initial count n2 and
\ limit n1. Exit immediately if n1 = n2.
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (?DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 'LEAVE ! 0 , NO-PEEP HERE
  ; IMMEDIATE

: LEAVE ( --- )
\ Runtime: leave the matching DO LOOP immediately.
\ All places where a leave address for the loop is needed are in a linked
\ list, starting with 'LEAVE variable, the other links in the cells where
\ the leave addresses will come.
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LEAVE) HERE 'LEAVE @ , 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP ; IMMEDIATE

: RESOLVE-LEAVE
\ Resolve the references to the leave addresses of the loop.
\
\ Each cell gets HERE MINUS ITS OWN ADDRESS, not HERE: the operand is
\ relative, so that a word compiled at run time and then saved by
\ SAVE-SYSTEM still works when the image reloads at a different base.
\ (?DO) and (LEAVE) add the cell's address back.
          'LEAVE @
          BEGIN DUP WHILE DUP @ SWAP HERE OVER - SWAP ! REPEAT DROP ;

: LOOP  ( x --- )
\ End a DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: Add 1 to the count and if it is equal to the limit leave the loop.
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP
  ; IMMEDIATE

: +LOOP ( x --- )
\ End a DO +LOOP
\ Runtime: ( n ---) Add n to the count and exit if this crosses the
\ boundary between limit-1 and limit.
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (+LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP
  ; IMMEDIATE

: RECURSE ( --- )
\ Compile a call to the current (not yet finished) definition.
  LAST @ NAME> CALL, ; IMMEDIATE

: ."  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: type that string.
   POSTPONE (.") 34 WORD C@ 1+ ALLOT ALIGN ; IMMEDIATE


: S"  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: ( --- c-addr u) Return start address and length of that string.
  STATE @ IF POSTPONE (S") 34 WORD C@ 1+ ALLOT ALIGN
             ELSE 34 WORD COUNT POCKET PLACE POCKET COUNT THEN ; IMMEDIATE

: ABORT"  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: ( f --- ) if f is nonzero, print the string and abort program.
  POSTPONE (ABORT") 34 WORD C@ 1+ ALLOT ALIGN ; IMMEDIATE

: ABORT ( --- )
\ Abort unconditionally without a message.
1 ABORT" " ;

: POSTPONE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Parse the next word delimited by spaces and compile the following runtime.
\ Runtime: depending on immediateness EXECUTE or compile the execution
\ semantics of the parsed word.
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (POSTPONE) ' HERE - , NO-PEEP ; IMMEDIATE

: IMMEDIATE ( --- )
\ Make last definition immediate, so that it will be executed even in
\ compilation mode.
  LAST @ DUP C@ 64 OR SWAP C! ;

: ( ( "ccc<rparen>" --- )
\ Comment till next ).
  BEGIN
    41 DUP PARSE + C@ = 0=
\    [CHAR] ) DUP PARSE + C@ = 0=
  WHILE
    REFILL 0= IF EXIT THEN
  REPEAT
; IMMEDIATE

: \
\ Comment till end of line.
  SOURCE >IN ! DROP ; IMMEDIATE

: >BODY ( xt --- a-addr)
\ Convert execution token to parameter field address.
  CELL+ ;

: (;CODE) ( --- )
\ Runtime for DOES>: make the last created word call the code after
\ the call to this word, and end the defining word (R> discards its
\ return address). The body becomes [DODOES][call to the tail]; CREATE
\ left room for that, and the three-byte form still fits because the
\ body is cell-aligned. ALIGNED because DOES> pads the tail to a call
\ target.
  R> ALIGNED LAST @ NAME>             ( tail xt )
  DODOES-TOK OVER C! 1+ SWAP          ( a-addr tail )
  START @ - CELLSHIFT-TOK RSHIFT      ( a-addr v )
  DUP 16384 < IF
    2DUP 255 AND SWAP 1+ C!  8 RSHIFT 128 OR SWAP C!
  ELSE
    2DUP 255 AND SWAP 2 + C!  2DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND SWAP 1+ C!
    16 RSHIFT 192 OR SWAP C!
  THEN ;

: DOES>  ( --- )
\ Word that contains DOES> will change the behavior of the last created
\ word such that it pushes its parameter field address onto the stack
\ and then executes whatever comes after DOES>. The tail must start on
\ a call target, so it is padded; the pad is never executed. DODOES
\ leaves the parameter field on the return stack for the tail's R>.
  POSTPONE (;CODE) ALIGN NO-PEEP POSTPONE R> ; IMMEDIATE

\ PART 10: TOP LEVEL OF INTERPRETER

: ?STACK ( ---)
\ Check for stack over/underflow and abort with an error if needed.
  DEPTH DUP 0< SWAP 10000 > OR ABORT" Stack error" ;

: INTERPRET ( ---)
\ Interpret words from the current source until the input source is exhausted.
  BEGIN
   32 WORD DUP C@
  WHILE
   FIND DUP
   IF
    -1 = STATE @ AND
    IF
     COMPILE,
    ELSE
     EXECUTE
    THEN
   ELSE DROP
    NUMBER? 0= ABORT" Undefined word" DROP
    STATE @ IF LITERAL THEN
   THEN  ?STACK
  REPEAT   DROP
;

: EVALUATE ( c-addr u --- )
\ Evaluate the string c-addr u as if it were typed on the terminal.
  SID @ >R SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R  >IN @ >R
  #SRC ! SRC ! 0 >IN ! -1 SID ! INTERPRET
  R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC ! R> SID ! ;

VARIABLE INCLUDE-BUFFER ( --- a-addr)
\ This is the buffer where the lines of included files are stored.
508 ALLOT-T

VARIABLE INCLUDE-POINTER ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable holds the address where the included line is stored.

: INCLUDE-FILE ( fid --- )
\ Read lines from the file identified by fid and interpret them.
\ INCLUDE and EVALUATE nest in arbitrary order.
  INCLUDE-POINTER @ >R SID @ >R SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R >IN @ >R
  #SRC @ INCLUDE-POINTER +! INCLUDE-POINTER @ SRC !
  SID !
  BEGIN
   REFILL
  WHILE
   INTERPRET
  REPEAT
  R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC ! R> SID ! R> INCLUDE-POINTER !
;

: INCLUDED  ( c-addr u ---- )
\ Open the file with name c-addr u and interpret all lines contained in it.
  R/O OPEN-FILE ABORT" Can't open include file"
  DUP >R INCLUDE-FILE
  R> CLOSE-FILE DROP
;

: QUIT ( --- )
\ This word resets the return stack, resets the compiler state, the include
\ buffer and then it reads and interprets terminal input.
  R0 @ RP! [
  TIB SRC ! 0 SID !
  INCLUDE-BUFFER INCLUDE-POINTER !
  BEGIN
   REFILL DROP INTERPRET STATE @ 0= IF ." OK" THEN CR
  0 UNTIL
;

: WARM ( ---)
\ This word is called when an error occurs. Clears the stacks, sets
\ BASE to decimal, closes the files and resets the search order.
  R0 @ RP! S0 @ SP! DECIMAL
  2 #ORDER !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT CELL+ !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CURRENT !
  QUIT ;

: RELOCATE-WORDLIST ( wid --- )
\ Add START to every thread head of one wordlist.
\
\ Cell 0 is the thread COUNT and must not be touched; an empty thread
\ is 0 and must STAY 0, or it becomes a pointer to the image base.
\ SAVE-SYSTEM's SS-UNRELOCATE-WORDLIST is this in reverse and the two
\ have to agree.
  DUP @ 1+ 1 DO
    DUP I CELLS + DUP @ ?DUP IF START @ + SWAP ! ELSE DROP THEN
  LOOP DROP ;

: COLD ( --- )
\ The first word that is called at the start of Forth.
  START ! START @ DP +!
  FORTH-WORDLIST RELOCATE-WORDLIST
  \ ...and every other wordlist, down the chain WORDLIST built.
  WORDLISTS @
  BEGIN ?DUP WHILE
    START @ + DUP RELOCATE-WORDLIST
    1 CELLS - @
  REPEAT
  SP@ S0 !
  RP@ CELL+ R0 ! \ Initialize variables SO and RO
  BOOT @ ?DUP IF START @ + EXECUTE THEN
  \ A turnkey image's own boot word normally never returns (shell.4's
  \ MAIN either exits or runs its own read-eval loop). If one does
  \ return, fall through to the ordinary interactive behavior rather
  \ than doing something surprising.
  ." Welcome to Forth " CR
  WARM ;

END-CROSS
